<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Publius Sulpicius Quirinius</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 21 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a> · Also known as Quirinius</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Publius Sulpicius Quirinius was a Roman senator, consul in 12 BCE, who as governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> carried out a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaea">Judaea</a> in 6 CE, when it became a Roman province after the deposition of Archelaus. The census provoked the revolt of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judas-of-galilee">Judas of Galilee</a> and is mentioned in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospel-of-luke">Gospel of Luke</a>. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 21 CE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/publius-sulpicius-quirinius.jpg" alt="Publius Sulpicius Quirinius" width="320" height="221" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Master of the Church of the Holy Saviour in Chora, Istanbul · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Meister_der_Kahriye-Cami-Kirche_in_Istanbul_005.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
